    "result_text": "The build is green. All 261 tests pass, including both acceptance tests (`AcceptanceTest`: 2/2), which cover the new postcode-preference behavior.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe locality derivation lived in `OwnerMapper.formatLocality` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`) and previously derived the region from `city` only, ignoring `postcode`. This made `functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity` (unknown city + VIC-range postcode `3000` \u2192 expected `VIC`) fail.\n\nI changed `formatLocality` to **prefer the postcode**:\n\n1. Added a private helper `regionForPostcode(String)` that maps a 4-digit postcode to a region by inclusive range \u2014 NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099 \u2014 and returns `null` when the postcode is absent, malformed, or in no known range.\n2. `formatLocality` now resolves via `regionForPostcode` first and only falls back to the existing city-to-region switch (`Sydney\u2192NSW`, `Melbourne\u2192VIC`, `Brisbane\u2192QLD`, else `UNKNOWN`) when the postcode yields no region.\n\nThis returns the same region for known cities (their valid postcodes fall in the matching range) while disambiguating same-named cities via the postcode. The ranges match the validation table already used in `OwnerRestControllerV1` and the test fixtures. I confirmed `postcode` round-trips through the create\u2192read REST path under the active `spring-data-jpa`/HSQLDB test profile, so the mapper sees the persisted value.\n\n`./mvnw test` \u2192 **261 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS.**",
